A||||7||lowfunds||removing egr valve and tube?||||1||
Z||000000||lowfunds||08-12-2001||10:08 PM||||Has anyone removed the EGR valve and tube and close them off. have a broken tube and don't have the money to fix right now. Other than emissions and possible detonation are there other issues? Don't plan on leaving it this way just wondering if it has been done.||||reg||1
Z||000001||BlackDragon||08-12-2001||10:24 PM||||shouldnt hurt anything for a short period, if you are going to do it for long, you will need to buy high octane fuel though||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fieroist||08-12-2001||10:29 PM||||I left the valve and tube off of one v-6 I rebuilt, but I had the upper intake plenum off and was able to pipe thread and plug the manifold where the tube connected. (also need to put the tube mounting bolts back in since one of them is drilled all the way through) This car had the exhaust crossover pipe welded shut where the egr valve used to mount. You could leave the egr valve on and remove and plug the vacuum line going to it though- then be able to install the tube later. I didn't notice any increase in pinging or other problems with this engine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowfunds||08-12-2001||10:47 PM||||What about removing it altogether? I just read in another thread about using an asd chip will allow you to do this. And it could be had for the same price as a new EGR tube $100 WOW.... <p>[This message has been edited by lowfunds (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||08-12-2001||11:26 PM||||Many people say it won't hurt anything, while others say you run the risk of detonation and burning a piston.  Most of the people who say it's ok, probably haven't torn their engine down to inspect it.  I'm currently running without an EGR on mine, until I can get a new pipe.  As a precaution, I'm running premium fuel.  I can't tell a difference in how it runs.  It did pass an emission inspection with the EGR blocked off, though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||08-13-2001||09:11 PM||||This is puzzling. If the factory computer can compensate for headers and exhaust, high flow intake, 1.6 ratio rockers, head porting and a mild cam without melting pistons, how is it possible that it can't compensate for a blocked off EGR? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowfunds||08-13-2001||09:51 PM||||That's kind of what I was wondering. My understanding is the ECM will automatically advance the timing if the EGR is removed leading to detonation. Anyone else with some more imput on this topic?||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||08-13-2001||10:10 PM||||The timing is advanced when the EGR is active. The fuel is adjusted too. Since the EGR is not there, the added advance can push you over the ping line. It goes like this...<P>%EGR-&gt;Degrees advance adder (read as %EGR gives n degrees of extra advance).<P>   0-&gt;0<BR> 1.6-&gt;1.75<BR> 3.2-&gt;3.16<BR> 4.8-&gt;4.21<BR> 6.4-&gt;5.97<BR> 8.0-&gt;7.03<BR> 9.6-&gt;8.78<BR>11.2-&gt;9.49<BR>12.8-&gt;9.49<BR>14.4-&gt;9.49<BR>16.0-&gt;9.49<BR>17.6-&gt;9.49<BR>19.2-&gt;9.49<BR>20.8-&gt;9.49<BR>22.4-&gt;9.49<BR>24.0-&gt;9.49<BR>25.6-&gt;9.49<P>10 degrees is a fair amount of cruise advance adder.<P>It tends to ping at part throttle since the EGR will close at WOT. Actually, EGR comes on at 3.9%% throttle and goes off when you get to 6.2%. It's also enabled at 68F CTS.<P>If the ping as severe (you ignore it and it's rattling like ball-bearings), you do risk damage, but the odds are low. Don't take that to the bank.<P>Cliff, can you make the message editor VI compatible?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>TK<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
